    {
      "id": "gW3cX8RFlXDoBI5OI9_t_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 600.3928571428568,
      "y": -1284.1190476190477,
      "width": 915.5195312500002,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "Z23yexhWsMba6t6AWkSub",
      "index": "b2OzzV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 747266263,
      "version": 837,
      "versionNonce": 1785425529,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777269079470,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The given size of both class Y and class Z on any machine is the interplay of three factors:\n1. Language support overhead.\nThere is an associated overhead incurred in the language support of virtual base classes.\nWithin the derived class, this overhead is reflected as some form of pointer, either to the\nvirtual base class subobject or to an associated table within which either the address or\noffset to the virtual base class subobject is stored. On my correspondent's machine, the\npointer is 4 bytes. (Virtual base classes are discussed in Section 3.4.)\n2. Compiler optimization of recognized special cases.\nThere is the 1 byte size of the virtual base class X subobject also present within Y (and Z).\nTraditionally, this is placed at the end of the \"fixed\" (that is, invariant) portion of the\nderived class. Some compilers now provide special support for an empty virtual base class\n(the paragraph following item 3 discusses this in more detail). Our correspondent's compiler,\nhowever, did not provide this special handling.\n3. Alignment constraints. \nThe size of class Y (and Z) at this point is 5 bytes. On most machines, aggregate\nstructures have an alignment constraint so that they can be efficiently loaded from and\nstored to memory. On my correspondent's machine, alignment of an aggregate is on a 4-byte\nboundary. So class Y (and Z) requires 3 bytes of padding. The result is a final size of 8.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The given size of both class Y and class Z on any machine is the interplay of three factors:\n1. Language support overhead.\nThere is an associated overhead incurred in the language support of virtual base classes. Within the derived class, this overhead is reflected as some form of pointer, either to the virtual base class subobject or to an associated table within which either the address or offset to the virtual base class subobject is stored. On my correspondent's machine, the pointer is 4 bytes. (Virtual base classes are discussed in Section 3.4.)\n2. Compiler optimization of recognized special cases.\nThere is the 1 byte size of the virtual base class X subobject also present within Y (and Z). Traditionally, this is placed at the end of the \"fixed\" (that is, invariant) portion of the derived class. Some compilers now provide special support for an empty virtual base class (the paragraph following item 3 discusses this in more detail). Our correspondent's compiler, however, did not provide this special handling.\n3. Alignment constraints. \nThe size of class Y (and Z) at this point is 5 bytes. On most machines, aggregate structures have an alignment constraint so that they can be efficiently loaded from and stored to memory. On my correspondent's machine, alignment of an aggregate is on a 4-byte boundary. So class Y (and Z) requires 3 bytes of padding. The result is a final size of 8.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
